\title{Justification of the name of global elements}
\taxon{Proposition}
\author{hsuchingchung}
\import{macros-subobj}
\date{2026-10-30T14:02:49Z}

\p{Fix an object #{A} in a category \CCat with a [terminal object](0005) #{1}.}

\p{An assignment #{(a_\Gamma)_\Gamma} of a [generalized element](0002) #{a_\Gamma : \Gamma \r A} of #{A} for each object #{\Gamma} in \CCat is called a \strong{global assignment of generalized elements of #{A}} if it is \em{invariant under context substitution} in the sense that:

##{a_\Delta \sigma = a_\Gamma}

for all morphisms #{\sigma : \Gamma \r \Delta} in \CCat.}


\p{Show that there is a one-to-one correspondence between global elements #{c : 1 \r A} of #{A} and global assignments of generalized elements #{(a_\Gamma)_\Gamma} of #{A}.
}

\subtree{
\taxon{Proof}

\p{

Given a global element #{c : 1 \r A} of #{A}, we can consider the assignment of generalized elements of #{A}:

##{c \bang_\Gamma : \Gamma \r 1 \r A}

for each object #{\Gamma}, where the morphism #{\bang_\Gamma : \Gamma \r 1} is the unique morphism into the [terminal object](0005) #{1}. Then #{(c \bang_\Gamma)_\Gamma} is invariant under context substitution since for any morphism #{\sigma: \Gamma \r \Delta}, we have #{\bang_\Gamma = \bang_\Delta \sigma} by the uniqueness of #{\bang_\Gamma}, and so #{c \bang_\Gamma = c \bang_\Delta \sigma}. 
}

\p{
Conversely, let #{(a_\Gamma)_\Gamma} be a global assignment of generalized elements of #{A}. We have a global element 

##{ a_1 : 1 \r A}

of #{A}.}

\p{
We check that these two constructions are inverses of each other:

\ul{
  \li{#{c \bang_1 = c}: Since #{\bang_1 = \id_1 : 1 \r 1} by the uniqueness of #{\bang_1}, we have #{c \bang_1 = c \id_1 = c}.}

  \li{#{a_1 \bang_\Gamma = a_\Gamma}: Since #{(a_\Gamma)_\Gamma} is "invariant under context substitution", taking #{\sigma := \bang_\Gamma : \Gamma \r 1} gives #{a_1 \bang_\Gamma = a_\Gamma}.}
}
}}
